WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 149: Chester A. Arthur and the Noble Transformation: From Spoilsman to Statesman

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.000
[Rebecca] Today, we examine one of the greatest redemptive arcs in the history of American statecraft.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.560
[Rebecca] The presidency of Chester Alan Arthur.

5
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:28.640
[Rebecca] Regarded by his contemporaries as nothing more than a cynical machine boss and a gilded Manhattan dandy, Arthur was ennobled by the heavy mantle of the presidency, sacrificing his party friendships to serve the highest ideals of the Republic.

6
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.440
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

7
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:58.240
[George] Editorial cartoons depicted Arthur as a smirking Tammany style boss handing over the keys of the Federal Treasury to his political patron, Senator Roscoe Conkling.

8
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:05.760
[George] A respected New York attorney who knew Arthur well exclaimed in horror, Chet Arthur, President of the United States.

9
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.440
[George] Good God, even Arthur himself was terrified.

10
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:26.240
[George] When Garfield was shot by Charles Guitau, who screamed Arthur's name as his motivation, Arthur locked himself inside his brownstone on Lexington Avenue in New York City, weeping in seclusion, afraid to appear in public for fear of being accused of complicity in the murder.

11
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.360
[George] Arthur's public reputation was that of the quintessential gilded age gentleman boss.

12
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:40.800
[George] Born in Fairfield, Vermont, the son of an abolitionist Baptist preacher, Arthur had risen as a brilliant organizer in the New York Republican machine.

13
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.800
[George] Tall, handsome, with immaculate mutton chop whiskers, Arthur was an unabashed dandy.

14
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:59.520
[George] He owned 80 pairs of custom tailored trousers, changed his silk ties several times a day, wore velvet collars, and relished midnight champagne dinners at Delmonico's that lasted until two in the morning.

15
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:11.000
[George] For seven lucrative years, he had presided over the New York Custom House, ensuring that millions of dollars in federal patronage flowed directly to Roscoe Conkling's political army.

16
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.400
[George] Yet beneath the veneer of the cynical Manhattan Bond vivant lay a deeply lonely, grieving man.

17
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:33.480
[George] In January 1880, just a year before his elevation to the vice presidency, Arthur's beloved wife Ellen Nell Herndon, the daughter of a heroic naval officer who had gone down with the SS Central America, died suddenly of pneumonia at age 42.

18
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:40.200
[George] Arthur was devastated, keeping her room untouched and placing fresh flowers before her portrait every day.

19
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:48.440
[George] It was into this deep emotional wound that an extraordinary series of letters arrived, providing the moral compass that would guide his presidency.

20
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:57.480
[George] The letters came from Julia Sand, a 31-year-old bedridden invalid living in a townhouse on East 74th Street in New York City.

21
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:06.280
[George] Sands suffered from severe spinal affliction, but possessed a razor-sharp intellect, deep moral passion, and fearless literary prose.

22
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:16.1000
[George] Beginning days after Garfield was shot, Sand wrote long, intimate letters to Arthur, treating him not as a corrupt politician, but as a man capable of supreme greatness.

23
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.680
[George] The nation is in mourning, but you have the power to turn its grief into honor, Sand wrote.

24
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.920
[George] Great emergencies awaken generous instincts in our noble natures.

25
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.400
[George] The presidency can be your crucible.

26
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.120
[George] Show the world that Chet Arthur the machine politician is dead, and Chester A.

27
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.560
[George] Arthur, the president of all the people has been born.

28
00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:43.160
[George] Arthur kept Sands's letters on his desk, reading them repeatedly during his darkest nights.

29
00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:53.840
[George] At 2, 15 on the morning of September 20, 1881, a messenger banged on the wooden front door of Arthur's townhouse at 123 Lexington Avenue.

30
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.280
[George] Garfield had died two hours earlier in Elberen.

31
00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:05.920
[George] Standing in his front parlor by the dim glow of gas lamps, surrounded by his son, his attorney, and a handful of friends, Chester A.

32
00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:12.080
[George] Arthur raised his right hand and took the presidential oath administered by New York Supreme Court Justice John R.

33
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:12.960
[George] Brady.

34
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.040
[George] Two days later, a second formal inauguration was held in the Vice President's room at the Capitol.

35
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:28.480
[George] In his brief inaugural address, delivered with solemn, tearful gravity, Arthur pledged to honor Garfield's memory and govern with impartial fairness.

36
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:42.960
[George] Senator Roscoe Conkling immediately descended on Washington, anticipating that his protege would dismiss Garfield's cabinet, reverse Hayes's civil service reforms, and hand over the patronage of the federal government to the stalwart faction.

37
00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:49.600
[George] But when Conkling walked into the White House expecting to dictate appointments, he met an entirely different man.

38
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:50.480
[George] Chester A.

39
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.800
[George] Arthur politely, but firmly, refused to be Conkling's puppet.

40
00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:03.520
[George] When Conkling demanded that Arthur remove Secretary of the Treasury William Wyndham and replace him with a stalwart crony, Arthur refused.

41
00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:11.120
[George] Conkling was stunned and furious, storming out of the executive mansion and declaring that Arthur had betrayed his oldest friends.

42
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.600
[George] The two men never spoke again.

43
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:22.400
[George] When Arthur moved into the executive mansion in late 1881, the fastidious Dandy was appalled by the filthy, rundown state of the house.

44
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:29.960
[George] Furniture was threadbare, carpets were stained with tobacco spit, and the basement was packed with decades of decaying junk.

45
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.760
[George] Arthur flatly refused to sleep in the building.

46
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.280
[George] I will not live in a house that looks like a badly kept barracks, he told his staff.

47
00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:43.480
[George] Go out and find me twenty-four wagon loads of this trash and sell it at public auction.

48
00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:53.560
[George] Arthur hired Louis Comfort Tiffany, the brilliant young master of the American Art Nouveau and aesthetic movements, to completely redesign the White House interior.

49
00:05:53.560 --> 00:06:04.040
[George] Tiffany installed a magnificent, fifty-foot-long screen of opalescent colored glass in the entrance hall, separating the public corridor from the private family quarters.

50
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:15.320
[George] The blue room and state dining room were repainted in luxurious jewel tones, outfitted with silverleaf wallpapers, electric lighting fixtures, and elegant Victorian furnishings.

51
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:25.640
[George] Arthur brought in a French chef, revived formal state dinners with exquisite menus, and restored an atmosphere of quiet European sophistication to the White House.

52
00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:34.360
[George] Yet every evening, before retiring to bed, Arthur walked into the red room and placed fresh flowers beneath the portrait of his late wife Nell.

53
00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.080
[George] The supreme test of Arthur's presidency arrived in the winter of 1882.

54
00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:46.520
[George] Following the midterm elections, in which voters punished the Republican Party for Garfield's assassination, Senator George H.

55
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:50.440
[George] Pendleton of Ohio introduced the Civil Service Reform Act.

56
00:06:50.440 --> 00:07:02.520
[George] The bill proposed creating a bipartisan civil service commission, banning political campaign assessments on federal employees, and requiring open, competitive examinations for federal jobs.

57
00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:10.040
[George] Skeptics assumed that Arthur, who had made his career distributing spoils, would veto the bill or quietly sabotage it.

58
00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:19.200
[George] Instead, in his annual message to Congress, President Arthur surprised friends and enemies alike by urging Congress to pass the legislation immediately.

59
00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.200
[George] On January 16, 1883, Chester A.

60
00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.640
[George] Arthur signed the Pendleton Civil Service Act into law.

61
00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.440
[George] It was an act of profound political courage.

62
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.760
[George] By signing the act, Arthur voluntarily dismantled the very patronage machine that had elevated him to power.

63
00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:37.600
[George] He appointed Dorman B.

64
00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:47.440
[George] Eaton, a staunch civil service crusader who had once been assaulted on a street corner by Conkling's thugs, as the first chairman of the Civil Service Commission.

65
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:56.880
[George] Arthur rigorously enforced the merit system, ensuring that qualifications and competence, rather than party loyalty, governed federal employment.

66
00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:02.720
[George] The spoil system that had dominated American politics since Andrew Jackson was mortally wounded.

67
00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:06.800
[George] Arthur's vision extended far beyond civil service reform.

68
00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.000
[George] In 1881, the United States Navy was an international laughingstock.

69
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:22.560
[George] While European powers were constructing massive steam-powered, armored ironclads, the American fleet consisted of rotting wooden sailing vessels left over from the Civil War.

70
00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:35.760
[George] A prominent naval report revealed that of the Navy's 140 ships, only 25 were fit for active duty, and some carried smoothbore cannon that would bounce harmlessly off the armor of modern European cruisers.

71
00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.720
[George] The United States was incapable of defending even its own Atlantic coastline.

72
00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.360
[George] Partnering with Secretary of the Navy William E.

73
00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:48.480
[George] Chandler, Arthur went to Congress to demand the construction of a modern steel fleet.

74
00:08:48.480 --> 00:09:01.720
[George] In 1883, over fierce congressional opposition, Arthur secured funding for the ABCD ships, the modern steel cruisers Atlanta, Boston, and Chicago, and the dispatch boat Dolphin.

75
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:12.600
[George] These vessels were constructed entirely of domestic American steel, stimulating the growth of the American metallurgical industry and marking the birth of the modern United States Navy.

76
00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.240
[George] The steel fleet commissioned by Chester A.

77
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:26.280
[George] Arthur would, fifteen years later, steam across the globe to secure victory in the Spanish American War and establish the United States as a premier global naval power.

78
00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:30.200
[George] Throughout his presidency, Arthur harbored a fatal secret.

79
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:40.440
[George] In 1882, doctors diagnosed him with Bright's disease, a chronic, incurable kidney ailment that caused severe fatigue, weight loss, and edema.

80
00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:51.560
[George] Knowing that public disclosure of his illness would cause panic, Arthur suffered in silence, enduring bouts of extreme exhaustion while maintaining his demanding presidential schedule.

81
00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:59.880
[George] Knowing he was dying, Arthur made only a token effort for renomination in 1884, stepping aside gracefully to allow James G.

82
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:02.120
[George] Blaine to take the Republican banner.

83
00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:09.800
[George] One evening in August 1882, a carriage pulled up to the brownstone at 123 Lexington Avenue in New York.

84
00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:13.640
[George] A tall, handsome gentleman stepped out and knocked on the door.

85
00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:15.400
[George] It was President Chester A.

86
00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:23.000
[George] Arthur, coming to pay a personal visit to Julia Sand, the invalid woman whose letters had inspired his transformation.

87
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:31.640
[George] For an hour, the president sat in her parlor, speaking softly with the woman who had believed in his nobility when the rest of the nation had despised him.

88
00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:39.160
[George] Arthur retired to New York in March 1885 and died of a cerebral hemorrhage 20 months later at age 57.

89
00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] The legendary publisher Alexander McClure summarized his remarkable presidency with immortal eloquence.

90
00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:56.480
[George] No man ever entered the presidency so profoundly and widely distrusted, and no man ever retired from it more generally respected, alike by political friend and foe.

91
00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:57.920
[Rebecca] Chester A.

92
00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:07.120
[Rebecca] Arthur's presidency proved that the highest office in the Republic can ennoble a man, calling forth hidden reserves of dignity, independence, and moral courage.

93
00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.880
[Rebecca] By breaking with his past and championing merit-based governance, Arthur preserved the legacy of James A.

94
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.000
[Rebecca] Garfield and modernized the American state.

95
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:25.120
[Rebecca] Next week, we begin our exploration of the only president in American history to serve two non-consecutive terms, Grover Cleveland.

96
00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.280
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

